 *   **Limitations:** Requires synchronized RGB and event camera data; complex joint optimization; relies on SDF reconstruction which may be sensitive to initialization [S1, unofficial][S2].
 *   **When to use:** Dynamic scenes with fast motion where traditional RGB-based NeRF fails; applications requiring relighting of dynamic objects [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Static scenes; scenes without event camera data; real-time applications with limited computational resources [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## Background
-DE-NeRF was proposed to address the limitations of NeRF in dynamic scenes, specifically for rapidly moving and deforming objects [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principle of decomposing lighting, geometry, and appearance to allow relighting of high-frequency environments and appearance editing [S2]. The method integrates event cameras, which offer high temporal resolution, with sparse RGB frames to model dynamic scenes effectively [S1, unofficial]. It was cited in surveys on dynamic scene reconstruction and methods for improving novel view synthesis quality [S1, unofficial][S2].
